<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montreal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montreal is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, on an island in the St Lawrence River. Founded as Ville-Marie in 1642 by French Catholic settlers, it became the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> and surrendered to the British in 1760. Briefly capital of the Province of Canada in the 1840s, it was the economic capital of Canada for much of its history and remains a major French-speaking city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montreal.jpg" alt="Montreal" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quintin Soloviev · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montreal,_Quebec_skyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trois-Rivières was founded in 1634 and <b>Ville-Marie</b>, later Montreal, in 1642 by Paul de Chomedey de Maisonneuve and Jeanne Mance as a religious mission.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Exhibitions have since gone to Barcelona, Madrid, Bogotá, Santiago de Chile, North America, Sweden, New South Wales, <b>Montreal</b>, New York, Liverpool, Perth and Budapest, and the army has been described as the only set of historic artefacts besides the remains of the Titanic that can draw a crowd…</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Catholic converts moved to the mission villages of Kahnawake and Kanesatake near <b>Montreal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>The most popular version was written in 1908 by Robert Stanley Weir, a lawyer and Recorder of the City of <b>Montreal</b>; it was not a translation of the French.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada</a></li><li><blockquote>Playing for the Boston Bruins against the <b>Montreal</b> Canadiens, Willie O'Ree became the first Black player in the National Hockey League, despite being almost blind in one eye.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-18">18 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Continental troops under Richard Montgomery entered <b>Montreal</b> unopposed during the invasion of Canada.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
